  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH THURSDAY...The front is best seen with the current \ntemperature gradient across the region, stretching from Fairmont\nup through the metro and into northwest WI. The majority of any\nrain this morning has stayed to our south and east, and cloud\ncover will become mostly scattered by midday. Breezy WNW winds \nwill gust to around 25 to 30 mph today, otherwise it'll be a \nrelatively quiet couple days of weather. There is some patchy \nfog this morning, which may become a bit more widespread \ntomorrow night as lows drop a good 5 to 10 degrees.\n\nFRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY...A broad Slight Risk (level 2 out of\n5) for severe weather remains in place for Friday. The \nforecasted environment, characterized by dewpoints in the upper \n60s, veering low level winds, and modest midlevel lapse rates \nof ~7 C/km, will be capable of supporting organized convection. \nOne main limiting factor may be the stronger flow aloft becoming\nincreasingly displaced to the northwest of the surface front \nand pool of stronger instability to the south. This is why we \nmay see bulk shear values of only 35 or so knots, rather than \nthe 55+ kts behind the front. Storm mode favors initially broken\nclusters or potentially discrete cells that grow upscale into \nline segments. The main window also looks to be Friday evening, \nbetween 6pm - midnight, while the LLJ becomes most intense.\n\nThe other thing to note about Friday is the strong return flow \nassociated with the low pressure system. During the day, very \nbreezy southerly winds are expected with gusts up to around 35 \nmph in southwest MN and around 25 to 30 mph elsewhere.\n\nThe pressure gradient loosens through the day Saturday behind\nthe front, and although winds turn out of the NW, it is not \nexpected to be as breezy. The quiet weather likely will not last\nlong though as another potent wave moves across the central US \nat the start of next week. Winds crank out of the south again, \nprompting strong moisture advection as a stalled boundary \nbecomes overrun/modified by the developing low pressure system \nover the Dakotas. Just looking at the run-to-run change for \nvarious ensemble guidance, there is a clear upwards trend for \nrainfall potential. Another good indicator for confidence in an \nabnormal event is the the ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index producing\nvalues near ~0.8 for the QPF. The scale caps out at 1, so a \nvalue of 0.8 shows confidence on the higher end of the spectrum \nfor anomalously high QPF values compared to climate averages for\nmid September. For those familiar with the Shift of Tails, \ncurrent values are maxing out around 1, indicating that there is\nnot as high of a \"boom\" potential for this setup. Overall, \nthere is a good signal for another solid rainfall event for \nparts of the Upper MS Valley early next week, and it will be a \nmatter of watching the evolution of the shortwaves aloft this \nweekend to see where the axis of heaviest rainfall sets up.\n\nFollowing the rain chances and frontal passages, cooler air from\nthe northwest will advect in, likely prompting many their \nfirst craving for something pumpkin spice flavored. There is a \nbit of uncertainty with how much below average we drop, with the\nlatest NBM nudging afternoon highs up a couple degrees from the\nprevious run. Regardless, it looks like longer stints of fall-\nlike temperatures stick around as anomalously high heights \nremain over the Aleutian Islands, funneling cooler airmasses \ninto central Canada and the Great Lakes region. The 8-14 Day \nOutlook for temperatures highlights a 33-50% chance for below \nnormal over Minnesota and Wisconsin.",
